WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:04.710
Hopes are high that the 13th installment of
the popular slasher franchise will make it

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.700
to the big screen sooner rather than later.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:10.830
Until then, let's take a look back at all
the Jason Voorhees stories that never came

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:11.320
to life.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.580
These are the Friday the 13th projects you'll
never get to see.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.980
A crossover between A Nightmare on Elm Street
and Friday the 13th was first pitched in 1987.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.760
And while it took 16 years for Freddy vs.
Jason to become a reality, loads of scripts

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.660
were written to support a slew of potential
mashup ideas.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.980
According to Bloody Disgusting, a potential
script called Nightmare 13: Freddy Meets Jason

00:00:39.999 --> 00:00:44.960
wrapped Jason Voorhees' origin story right
into Freddy's, suggesting Freddy Krueger was

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.980
the Camp Crystal Lake counselor who drowned
Jason Voorhees.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.420
This angle gave Jason an intriguing motive
for becoming a killer and wanting to battle

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:52.920
Freddy.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.560
The script was reportedly rewritten to include
a battle scene between Freddy and Jason that

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.690
took place in a boxing ring in hell.

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.640
An actor would portray Ted Bundy as the ring
announcer.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.540
And guess who was supposed to sit right in
the front row?

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:06.260
Adolf Hitler.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.740
Perhaps it's for the best that we never got
to see this one.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:15.860
New Line Cinema pitched their own Freddy vs.
Jason idea in 1997, and the O.J. Simpson trial

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:17.600
inspired their version of the story.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:23.080
According to Flickering Myth, the script took
a similar approach to Wes Craven's New Nightmare,

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:27.120
adding a meta layer that explored Jason going
on trial for his many murders.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.280
"Are you trying to justify to me the fact
that you're an idiot?"

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:37.340
Freddy Krueger eventually enters the action
and attempts to use Jason to get out of dreamland

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:38.380
and into the real world.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.940
Well, it certainly would've been interesting
to watch Jason take the stand, much like Freddy

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:48.100
did in an episode of Freddy's Nightmares called
"No More Mr. Nice Guy," directed by Tobe Hooper.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.040
"Come on!

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.080
I'd rather burn up than fade away!

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:55.240
I'll be back…

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.560
I'm free… free!"

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:03.300
After the success of Platinum Dunes' Friday
the 13th reboot in 2009, Paramount and Warner

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.760
Brothers started working on a follow-up with
the studio.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.360
"Uh, are you looking for this?

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.900
'Cuz it, uh, it completes your outfit."

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:18.819
Bloody Disgusting reports a sequel called
Friday the 13th: Camp Blood was written in

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:23.780
2010 by Damian Shannon and Mark Swift, the
same guys who wrote the Freddy vs. Jason script.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.599
According to multiple rumors, Derek Mears
was going to play Jason again, the film would

00:02:34.599 --> 00:02:39.790
be shot in 3D, and the plan was to release
the film opposite Platinum Dunes' other genre

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:42.320
reboot: 2010's A Nightmare on Elm Street.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:45.620
"I'm your boyfriend now."

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:52.160
Producer Brad Fuller ultimately announced
the cancellation of the project on Twitter

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:52.660
in 2010, writing:

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:56.240
"It's not my call: I would drop everything
and make it today if they wanted it."

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.780
Seven years later, a never-before-seen image
was revealed on Shannon and Swift's Twitter

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.520
feed: The title page of their never-filmed
script.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.980
Looks like they were was planning to kill
off Jason once and for all.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:08.040
Yeah, right.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.060
"We weren't doing anything.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.160
We were just messing— "

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:19.659
In 2013, Christopher Nolan's Interstellar
managed to reignite Friday the 13th hopes.

00:03:19.659 --> 00:03:23.530
According to The Hollywood Reporter, Warner
Brothers gave its rights to the franchise

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:26.720
over to Paramount to have a stake in the science
fiction epic.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.250
David Bruckner, the director of the "Amateur
Night" installment of the 2012 horror anthology

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:33.500
film VHS, was brought on to direct.

00:03:33.849 --> 00:03:38.140
Nick Antosca, the screenwriter behind the
Hannibal film adaptation, signed on to write

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:38.640
the script.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.680
What they came up with sounds like it would
have been a lot of fun.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.560
It would take place in the '80s.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.420
Jason's mom would have a role in the film,
and it would allegedly focus on Jason's supernatural

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:48.920
mythology.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:54.459
Bruckner told Fangoria he wanted to make a
cross between slasher horror and slacker comedy:

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:59.480
"My take on it was that I wanted to do Dazed
and Confused meets Jason Voorhees, a genuine

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.580
last-day-of-school coming-of-age story."

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:06.800
Unfortunately, due to the poor box office
returns of the horror sequel Rings, Paramount

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.860
wound up slashing Friday the 13th right off
its lineup.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.180
Would the story of Jason Voorhees work on
television?

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.660
The CW's Mark Pedowitz clearly thought the
answer was "yes."

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:24.780
In 2015, the network announced they had a
Friday the 13th TV series in the works.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:29.940
Series creator Steven Long Mitchell described
the tone as, quote, "a cross between the first

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.700
season of True Detective and Twin Peaks on
acid."

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.880
That's an odd analogy, considering those shows
seem like they're on acid already.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:49.160
According to Mitchell, the story would have
followed a police detective looking for his

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:50.660
lost brother in Crystal Lake.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:55.900
The central mystery would revolve around whether
Jason was killing again or a copycat was on

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:56.400
the loose.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.320
Sadly, the CW ended up passing on the series.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:01.150
Pedowitz told Collider:

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:05.280
"We didn't believe it was a sustainable script
or a sustainable series.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.370
It was a very good pilot, but not a sustainable
series."

